The same investigation tightened the remaining vehicle sphere-map contract.
Snake has five reflective `spheremap` slots. Each source shader is opaque and
lit and uses its component base texture plus `EnvMap.bmp`. Source shininess is
10; ambient, specular, and emissive terms are black; and the authored gray
`ENVB` byte is 76.

Snake's vehicle tuning independently contains `SetShininess(0.3)`. Despite its
historical name, that command does not change the shader shininess field. The
reachable vehicle runtime converts it to `int(255 * 0.3) == 76` and stores that
byte as the environment-reflection intensity, exactly matching all five source
shader `ENVB` values.

At display time, the user vehicle updates `ROTV` only for continuous signed
longitudinal movement between 0.01 and 50 source distance units. Its first
component accumulates signed distance times 0.05 modulo one turn; its third
component carries the local camera/movement-axis angle. `ENVBLEND` is broadcast
every display: exterior uses the configured reflection byte, interior uses that
byte shifted right by two, and damage multiplies it by
`clamp(0.75 * life + 0.25, 0, 1)`, where life is current hit points divided by
designer hit points.

The available reference backend does not consume `REFL`, `ROTV`, or `ENVB` and
falls the `spheremap` shader name through its ordinary simple-material program.
No other reference backend supplies the missing reflection-sampling or blend
equation. Therefore the runtime parameter math is now source-backed, but the
actual sphere-map sampling/composition remains unproven. Keep
`vehicle-spheremap-master-not-reviewed` and the reflection-policy blocker until
that visual equation has independent evidence; do not replace it with an
invented metallic/roughness or generic Unreal reflection treatment.

The full accepted corpus contains 120 `spheremap` slots across 29 vehicles. All
29 corresponding physical-vehicle tuning records contain exactly one
`SetShininess` command; none are missing or duplicated. The authored values are
0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.19, 0.2, 0.25, 0.3, or 0.4, which map through the reachable
runtime conversion to reflection-intensity bytes 12, 25, 38, 48, 51, 63, 76,
or 102 respectively.

The current v5 Unreal vehicle-material evidence identifies `spheremap` and
retains the base texture plus common raster fields, but it does not yet project
the authored `REFL` texture identity or `ENVB` colour. `ROTV` is absent as
expected
from authored sidecars because the user-vehicle runtime derives it from motion
and camera state. A future evidence revision must preserve `REFL` and `ENVB`
and join the per-vehicle tuning value before native sphere-map construction can
be reviewed. Do not encode a synthetic authored `ROTV` field.

A full sidecar audit confirms the stricter source envelope: all 120 sphere-map
shaders retain both `REFL` and `ENVB`. All 120 reference `EnvMap.bmp` for
`REFL`. Authored `ENVB` is not a substitute for vehicle tuning. Only 10 of 120
sphere-map slots happen to match the byte produced by their vehicle's
`SetShininess` value; 110 differ.

The reachable display path broadcasts a new per-vehicle `ENVBLEND` every frame.
Treat shader `ENVB` as source shader state and `SetShininess` as the runtime
vehicle override. Snake's 76-to-76 match corroborates the conversion but does
not imply that both authored values are generally identical.
